  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $C.$ Hence, $B$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $B({x}_{B},{y}_{B},{z}_{B}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{B},{y}_{B})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $B$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/6d98be34c848e0fbce571fc572fe9dae244e6ce80b677063fea2e19983e1a2da.png)",
